  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "847bea20-3d8f-4fc6-9381-f9b9404aa5da",
      "name": "📌 Overview – How This Workflow Works",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -2016,
        1792
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 2,
        "width": 852,
        "height": 692,
        "content": "## 🧾 Parse Invoice File from Warehouse – Webhook Test Pipeline\n\n### How it works\nThis is the webhook intake and XML parsing sub-workflow for the warehouse invoice sync. Your warehouse ERP or middleware POSTs a binary XML invoice file to the webhook endpoint. The file is decoded from binary, the raw XML is set as a field, then converted to a structured JSON object. The loop then iterates over each invoice in the payload, searches GoHighLevel for the matching opportunity by order reference, and updates the opportunity with the invoiced amount, pipeline stage, and custom field values.\n\nThis workflow covers the **webhook + manual test path** only — the main invoice processing logic (header extraction, line matching, missed sale creation) lives in the primary Parse Invoice workflow.\n\n### Setup steps\n1. Point your warehouse ERP or Make.com scenario to the **Webhook** production URL.\n2. Replace `YOUR_GHL_API_TOKEN` in both HTTP nodes with your GHL Private Integration Token.\n3. Replace `YOUR_GHL_LOCATION_ID`, `YOUR_GHL_PIPELINE_ID`, `YOUR_GHL_PIPELINE_STAGE_ID`, and the three custom field IDs in the Update node.\n4. Test by sending a sample XML payload to the webhook URL.\n5. Activate the webhook trigger."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "ec59fcd4-741e-429e-950a-aab19e48c690",
      "name": "Section – Webhook Intake & XML Parsing",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -864,
        2288
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 940,
        "height": 472,
        "content": "## 📨 Webhook Intake & XML Parsing\nReceives the binary invoice XML, decodes it from binary, sets it as a raw string field, then converts the XML to a JSON object. All downstream nodes work with the parsed JSON — no raw XML handling needed after this point."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "4261560a-dd85-48fb-ba11-b4caf5e04e31",
      "name": "Section – GHL Opportunity Lookup & Update",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        128,
        2288
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 844,
        "height": 472,
        "content": "## 🔗 GHL Opportunity Lookup & Update (Test Path)\nLoops through each parsed invoice, searches GHL for the matching opportunity by order reference, then updates it with the invoiced monetary value, pipeline stage, and custom field values. Both HTTP nodes use placeholder credentials — replace before going live."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "f4c94568-694b-471f-a963-148294ee7d08",
      "name": "🔑 Credentials & Configuration",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        752,
        2848
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 3,
        "width": 524,
        "height": 328,
        "content": "## 🔑 Credentials & GHL IDs\n- **GHL Private Integration Token** → `YOUR_GHL_API_TOKEN`\n- Replace `YOUR_GHL_LOCATION_ID`, `YOUR_GHL_PIPELINE_ID`, `YOUR_GHL_PIPELINE_STAGE_ID`, and custom field IDs in the Update node.\n\nNever hardcode real tokens or IDs in shared templates."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
